Title: Diagnosis
Details: 
The failing test `testEscapeHtmlHighUnicode` tries to verify that `StringEscapeUtils.escapeHtml` correctly escapes a high Unicode character (codepoint U+1D362, which is above U+FFFF) to the corresponding numeric character reference `&#119650;`. The test constructs a string from the UTF-8 byte sequence for that character and then calls `escapeHtml`.

Looking at `StringEscapeUtils.escapeHtml(String)`, this method delegates to `escapeHtml(Writer, String)`, which in turn calls `Entities.HTML40.escape(Writer, String)`. The `Entities.escape(Writer, String)` implementation iterates through the string one `char` at a time, handling each character with the `entityName` method to determine if it is a named HTML entity, else encoding it to a numeric entity if above 0x7F:

```java
for (int i = 0; i < len; i++) {
    char c = str.charAt(i);
    String entityName = this.entityName(c);
    if (entityName == null) {
        if (c > 0x7F) {
            writer.write("&#");
            writer.write(Integer.toString(c, 10));
            writer.write(';');
        } else {
            writer.write(c);
        }
    } else {
        writer.write('&');
        writer.write(entityName);
        writer.write(';');
    }
}
```

The key issue is that in Java, characters above U+FFFF (called supplementary characters) are represented internally as two `char` values, called a surrogate pair. The current code processes each `char` independently, treating each surrogate half as a separate character. Hence, it converts the 2 `char` units into 2 separate numeric entities rather than one correct numeric entity representing the full codepoint.

Because of this, the test `testEscapeHtmlHighUnicode` fails, as `escapeHtml` returns two numeric entities instead of a single one for the full codepoint U+1D362.

The root cause is that the `Entities.escape` method does not handle surrogate pairs properly and instead escapes each `char` unit independently. Supporting supplementary Unicode characters requires recognizing pairs of surrogate chars and combining them into a single codepoint before escaping.

The current bug report description aligns perfectly with this diagnosis, stating that characters represented by two internal characters are incorrectly converted.

The recent bug reports are unrelated to this issue.

Summary: The bug occurs because `Entities.escape` treats surrogate pairs as two separate `char`s rather than one Unicode codepoint, causing incorrect escaping of characters above U+FFFF.

Next, the best classes and methods to fix this issue need to be identified.
